foreign the Bell Tolls for the 158  people who perished in October in one of  the worst accidents in South Korea's  recent history  families and relatives gathered at this  Buddhist temple in Seoul to remember  those they lost  words from bereaved family members are  read out  I miss you more with the passing of time  with every breath each of my joints  cries out you worked hard toward your  dream of having your own business now  you and your dream are gone  she paid tribute to her son Jihan who  was an actor  I've yet to register jihan's death I  don't think I ever can  please don't forget but remember our  sons and daughters whose lives were  ended on the cold etawan Pavement in  South Korea  49 days on the victim's family say the  government has not been clear in its  words and actions they want to know what  caused the crowd crash on October 29th  and accountability calling for the  minister of the Interior safety is hung  means his step down the presidential  office says it will wait until the  conclusion of police and parliamentary  investigations before deciding who's  responsible more than a dozen people in  the chain of command that night have  been charged with professional  negligence the police investigation is  focusing on why even as the first call  for help went out at 6 34 PM with some  warning of an impending crush it would  take more than four hours to block off  foot traffic from that bottlenecked  Alleyway and as the probes continue and  the deceased are mourned many are still  living with the guilt of having survived  that night one high school student who  survived the crush was found dead in an  apparent suicide late Monday night after  his mother had reported him missing he'd  lost two friends and had been receiving  counseling at school  Eunice Kim Al Jazeera Seoul
 

